  "id": "hip-thrust-bridge",
  "name": "Hip Thrust and Bridge",
  "definition": "A bilateral externally loaded closed-chain compound movement in which the feet remain planted, a padded barbell loads the pelvis, and the pelvis rises until the hips reach neutral. The reviewed barbell hip thrust uses an elevated upper-back anchor and ends near 90 degrees of knee flexion; the reviewed barbell glute bridge anchors the upper torso on the floor and ends near 115 degrees. Both involve hip extension and some knee extension, so this is not an isolated hip-extension family. Gluteus maximus is primary and the vasti are secondary; gluteus medius controls the pelvis, the visible unsplit biceps-femoris region receives only knee-control credit, and soleus is a conservative mechanics-derived ankle-and-foot stabilizer. Measured pelvic-trunk and ankle motion did not support position-held claims, so both are nonstandardized without promoting variable excursion or net joint moments to deliberate prime actions. Bodyweight, band, dumbbell, Smith, machine, unilateral, feet-elevated, frog-pump, abduction, and articulated-spine variants remain outside this contract.",
  "evidenceRefs": [
    "arnold-2010-lower-limb",
    "brazil-2021-barbell-hip-thrust",
    "kennedy-2024-hip-thrust-glute-bridge"
  ],
